\section*{APPENDIX: MODELING THE SHORT-RANGE PART}

In this appendix, we briefly recall how the short-range DP model is constructed. The short-range part \(E_{\mathrm{sr}}\) is given by a sum of atomic contributions,
\[
E_{\mathrm{sr}}=\sum_{I} E_{I}^{\mathrm{sr}}, \quad E_{I}^{\mathrm{sr}}=\mathcal{F}\left(\mathcal{D}\left(\mathcal{R}_{I}\right)\right)
\]
where \(\mathcal{R}_{I}\) is an environment matrix whose rows record the positions, relative to atom \(I\), of the neighbors of that atom, within a specified cutoff radius \(r_{c}\). We consider two sets of neighboring atoms \(\mathcal{N}_{I}^{a} =\left\{R_{J}:\left|R_{J}-R_{I}\right| \leq r_{c}^{a}, J \neq I\right\} \quad\) and \(\quad \mathcal{N}_{I}=\left\{R_{J}:\left|R_{J}-R_{I}\right| \leq r_{c}, J \neq I\right\}\), where \(r_{c}^{a}\) and \(r_{c}\) denote two cutoff radii, typically satisfying \(r_{c}^{a} \leq r_{c}\). The number of neighbors of atom \(I\) in the two sets is correspondingly denoted by \(N^{a}(I)\) and \(N(I)\). The two sets are used for constructing descriptors with angular and radial information.

The environment matrix \(\tilde{\mathcal{R}}_{I}^{a}\) is derived from the neighbor set \(\mathcal{N}^{a}(I)\) and has dimension equal to \(N^{a}(I) \times 4\), with each row being a four-dimensional vector \(s\left(R_{I J}\right) \times\left(1, x_{I J} /\left|R_{I J}\right|, z_{I J} /\left|R_{I J}\right|, z_{I J} /\left|R_{I J}\right|\right)\). Here, the relative positions are defined as \(R_{I J}=R_{I}-R_{J}\), and their Cartesian components are denoted by \(R_{I J}=\left(z_{I J}, y_{I J}, z_{I J}\right) . s\left(R_{I J}\right)\) is defined as \(w\left(\left|R_{I J}\right|\right) /\left|R_{I J}\right|\), with \(w\) being a switch function that decays smoothly from 1 to 0 at the cutoff radius. The embedding matrix \(\mathcal{G}_{I}^{a}\) has dimension equal to \(N^{a}(I) \times M^{a}\), with each row mapping \(s\left(R_{I J}\right)\) to an \(M^{a}\) dimensional vector via a feed forward DNN. A submatrix of \(\mathcal{G}_{I}^{a}\) that includes its first \(M_{1}\) columns ( \(M_{1}^{a}<M^{a}\) ) is denoted by \(\mathcal{G}_{I}^{a,<}\). The descriptor defined by \(\mathcal{D}^{a}=\frac{1}{\left(N^{a}(I)\right)^{2}}\left(\mathcal{G}_{I}^{a,<}\right)^{T} \tilde{\mathcal{R}}_{I}^{a}\left(\tilde{\mathcal{R}}_{I}^{a}\right)^{T} \mathcal{G}_{I}^{a}\) contains angular and radial information, and one proves that it provides a symmetry preserving representation of the local environment of atom \(I\).

The environment matrix \(\tilde{\mathcal{R}}_{I}\) includes only radial neighbor information. It is set up from the neighbor set \(\mathcal{N}_{I}\) and has dimension equal to \(N(I) \times 1\), with each row being \(s\left(R_{I J}\right)\). The corresponding embedding matrix is denoted by \(\mathcal{G}_{I}\) and has dimension equal to \(N(I) \times M\). It should be noted that the embedding net used to map \(s\left(R_{I J}\right)\) to \(\mathcal{G}_{I}\) is usually different from the one used to generate \(\mathcal{G}_{I}^{a}\). The descriptor with only radial information is defined as \(\mathcal{D}^{r}=\frac{1}{N(I)} \sum_{j}\left(\mathcal{G}_{I}\right)_{j k}\), i.e., it is the average of the embedding matrix \(\mathcal{G}_{I}\) with respect to its first index. The descriptor \(\mathcal{D}^{a}\) contains radial and angular information on the environment, while \(\mathcal{D}^{r}\) contains only radial information. Typically, combined angular and radial information is more important for close neighbors, while radial information alone is sufficient to describe the more distant neighbors. We thus define a hybrid descriptor as the concatenation of the radial and angular descriptors, i.e., \(\mathcal{D}=\left(\mathcal{D}^{a}, \mathcal{D}^{r}\right)\). Relative to the descriptor \(\mathcal{D}=\mathcal{D}^{a}\), the hybrid descriptor \(\mathcal{D}=\left(\mathcal{D}^{a}, \mathcal{D}^{r}\right)\) has usually a stronger ability of generalization.

The hybrid descriptor is proved to be a symmetry preserving representation of the local environment of atom \(I . \mathcal{F}\) in (A1) denotes the fitting network, which is implemented by a feed forward DNN with skip connections. The DNNs used in the standard DP construction are trained in an end-to-end way by stochastic gradient descent schemes such as Adam. \({ }^{64}\)

